---
// /standards — the alignment posture, in the four-rung vocabulary that keeps us
// honest: aligned / compatible / compliant / certified. Nothing on this page may
// claim the last two, and a unit test greps for it. Content is read from
// scripts/standards/matrix.ts, the SAME object /api/standards.json serves, so
// the page and the machine-readable copy cannot disagree.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StandardsPage from '../components/standards/StandardsPage.astro';
import { APPROVED_STATEMENT, MATRIX, PHASES, UNCERTAINTY_STATEMENT } from '../scripts/standards/matrix.ts';

const site = Astro.site!.href.replace(/\/$/, '');
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Standards alignment — Delta Climate Research',
  url: `${site}/standards`,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', url: `${site}/` },
  about: 'Standards alignment of an urban climate digital twin: OGC CityJSON, OGC API Features, data governance.',
};
const counts = MATRIX.reduce((m, r) => (m[r.posture] = (m[r.posture] ?? 0) + 1, m), {} as Record<string, number>);
---
<Base
  title="Standards alignment — Delta Climate Research"
  description="How the urban climate digital twin aligns with OGC, smart-city and data-governance standards — stated as alignment, never certification, with a machine-readable copy at /api/standards.json."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <StandardsPage
    kicker="Standards · Alignment, not certification"
    title="Where the digital twin stands against the standards it targets"
    lede="Every row below says what a reader can go and fetch today, and what would be needed to move up. Four words carry the whole page, and only the first two apply to us."
  >
    <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Applies today</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Aligned</td><td>Implements the data model, schema or API pattern the standard defines</td><td>Yes — {counts.aligned ?? 0} standards</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compatible</td><td>Interoperates with a named ecosystem in testing</td><td>Not yet claimed for any</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compliant</td><td>Meets every normative requirement of the standard</td><td class="muted">No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certified</td><td>Formally assessed and approved by the standards body or authority</td><td class="muted">No</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Every external statement Delta makes sits in the first two rows unless a certificate exists. The approved public statement:</p>
    <blockquote><p>{APPROVED_STATEMENT}</p></blockquote>

    <h2>The matrix</h2>
    <p>Same data as <a href="/api/standards.json"><code>/api/standards.json</code></a>. {counts.aligned ?? 0} aligned, {counts.roadmap ?? 0} roadmap.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Posture</th><th>What ships</th><th>What would move it up</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {MATRIX.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.standard}<br /><span class="muted">{r.region} · {r.purpose}</span></td>
            <td><span class={`tag tag--${r.posture}`}>{r.posture}</span></td>
            <td>{r.ships}</td>
            <td class="muted">{r.gap}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>What is actually served</h2>
    <p>These are static files emitted at build time at the paths the OGC patterns use. There is no server behind them — which is stated rather than dressed up: read-only JSON has no rate limit or query validation because it has no query surface.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/api/standards.json"><code>/api/standards.json</code></a> — this matrix, machine-readable</li>
      <li><a href="/api/collections/wards.json"><code>/api/collections/wards.json</code></a> · <a href="/api/collections/wards/items.json"><code>…/items.json</code></a> · <code>…/items/&#123;id&#125;.json</code> — OGC API Features-inspired GeoJSON</li>
      <li><code>/api/wards/&#123;id&#125;/metadata.json</code> — a ward's measured confidence and full data lineage (<a href="/api/wards/ballygunge/metadata.json">ballygunge</a>)</li>
      <li><code>/api/wards/&#123;id&#125;/cityjson.json</code> — LoD1 CityJSON 2.0, one Building per shipped footprint, <strong>validated against the official 2.0 JSON Schema</strong> on every build (<a href="/api/wards/ballygunge/cityjson.json">ballygunge</a>, ~2.8 MB)</li>
      <li><code>/3d-tiles/&#123;id&#125;/tileset.json</code> — OGC 3D Tiles 1.1, the same buildings as a streamable glTF tileset for Cesium and deck.gl, <strong>0 errors from the official Cesium validator</strong> (<a href="/3d-tiles/ballygunge/tileset.json">ballygunge</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every payload carries <code>"status": "prototype"</code>, and every derived number in it traces to a line in <code>accuracy.ts</code> or a calibration artefact. The invented placeholder fields from the source specification (<code>population_exposed_high_heat</code>, <code>facade_glare_index</code>, <code>ac_energy_load_reduction_potential</code>) are pinned <em>out</em> by the unit tests.</p>

    <h2>The path from aligned to certified</h2>
    <p>Alignment is only worth stating if the route onward exists and our position on it is stated honestly. Every completed item below names the artefact that proves it — a tick with nothing behind it is marketing, not evidence.</p>
    {PHASES.map((ph) => {
      const done = ph.items.filter((i) => i.status === 'done').length;
      return (
        <>
          <h3>{ph.phase} — {ph.title} <span class="muted">· {done} of {ph.items.length} complete</span></h3>
          <p class="muted">{ph.objective}</p>
          <div class="tblwrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Status</th><th>Evidence / note</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {ph.items.map((i) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{i.item}</td>
                  <td><span class={`tag tag--${i.status === 'done' ? 'aligned' : i.status === 'partial' ? 'compatible' : 'roadmap'}`}>{i.status}</span></td>
                  <td class="muted">{i.evidence ?? '—'}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </>
      );
    })}
    <p>Phase 1 is complete. One Phase 2 item — validating CityJSON against the OGC schema — was brought forward and already passes on every build. Nothing here is certified, and nothing claims to be.</p>

    <h2>Governance</h2>
    <p>The posture is inspired by the Gemini Principles — open data, published error bars, published null results, no personal data — and it is a statement of principle, not an assessed conformance. The prototype processes no personal data: aggregated, ward-level, open geospatial inputs only. Formal legal review precedes any production deployment.</p>
    <p>See <a href="/uncertainty">/uncertainty</a> for the measured error and <a href="/attribution">/attribution</a> for every source and its licence.</p>

    <blockquote><p>{UNCERTAINTY_STATEMENT}</p></blockquote>
  </StandardsPage>
</Base>
